+# Overdue fines and member Accounts
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Returning a copy late costs the Member nothing, so DVDs in particular come back weeks after their due date. The circulation desk has no record of what a Member owes, staff have no way to charge a Member for anything, Members have no way to pay, and nothing stops a Member who owes a lot from borrowing more.
+
+## Solution
+
+Every Member gets an Account. When a Loan comes back late, the library automatically charges the Member a Fine for each Late day, up to the Title's replacement cost. Staff can add a Manual charge with a reason. Members can make a Payment of up to what they owe. The desk can look up any Member's Balance at any time. A Member whose Balance reaches the Borrowing limit (10.00) is Blocked from borrowing: checkout is refused until they pay the Balance down. Returning copies and paying always work, even for a Member who is blocked. Child Members are never fined.
+
+## User Stories
+
+### Fines on return
+
+1. As a librarian, I want a late return to charge the Member a Fine automatically, so that late returns finally cost something and copies come back on time.
+2. As a librarian, I want the Fine counted in Late days (days the library is open after the due date, up to and including the return day), so that Members are never fined for a day they couldn't have returned the copy.
+3. As a Member, I want Sundays never to count as Late days, so that I'm not charged for a day the library is closed.
+4. As a librarian, I want Late days to follow whatever days the library is closed, so that if opening days change, Fines follow without a separate rule.
+5. As a librarian, I want books fined at 0.25 per Late day, so that a book a week late costs about 1.50.
+6. As a librarian, I want DVDs fined at 1.00 per Late day, so that the short DVD loan is taken seriously.
+7. As a Member, I want a copy returned on or before its due date to cost nothing, so that returning on time is never penalised.
+8. As a Member, I want a copy returned one Late day after the due date to be fined that one day, so that the rule is simple and has no grace period to argue about.
+9. As a Member, I want the total fined for one Loan never to exceed the Title's replacement cost, so that a very late return never costs more than replacing the copy.
+10. As a parent, I want child Members never to be fined, so that children aren't put off using the library.
+11. As a librarian, I want a child's late return processed exactly like any other return (the Loan is closed and the copy goes back on the shelf), so that the only difference for children is that nothing is charged.
+12. As a librarian, I want each late return to add its own Fine, so that a Member who returns several copies late owes the sum of the Fines.
+13. As a librarian, I want returning a copy to give back the same Loan record it always has, so that the desk's existing return workflow keeps working.
+14. As a librarian, I want on-time returns and child returns to leave the Account untouched, so that the Account only records things the Member actually owes.
+15. As a librarian, I want a Loan that is still out and overdue to add nothing to the Balance until the copy comes back, so that the Balance only changes when something actually happens at the desk.
+
+### Balance
+
+16. As a librarian, I want to look up what a Member owes right now, so that I can tell them at the desk.
+17. As a Member, I want my Balance to be all my Charges minus all my Payments, so that I can check it adds up.
+18. As a librarian, I want looking up the Balance of an unknown Member to fail with the existing "member not found" error, so that typos in member ids are caught the same way everywhere.
+19. As a librarian, I want a newly registered Member to have a Balance of zero, so that new Members start with a clean Account.
+20. As a librarian, I want the Balance to never go negative, so that the library never owes Members credit.
+
+### Manual charges
+
+21. As a librarian, I want to add a Manual charge to a Member's Account with a reason, so that I can charge for things like a damaged case.
+22. As a librarian, I want adding a Manual charge to return the Member's new Balance, so that I can tell them straight away.
+23. As a librarian, I want to be able to add Manual charges to child Members, so that a child who damages a copy can still be charged for it.
+24. As a librarian, I want a Manual charge of zero or a negative amount to be refused as an invalid amount, so that a charge can't be used to reduce a Balance.
+25. As a librarian, I want a Manual charge that isn't in whole cents to be refused as an invalid amount, so that the Account never holds fractions of a cent.
+26. As a librarian, I want a Manual charge given as a float, an integer or a non-finite number to be refused as an invalid amount, so that money is always handled exactly.
+27. As a librarian, I want a Manual charge for an unknown Member to fail with "member not found" rather than "invalid amount", so that the more basic mistake is the one reported.
+28. As a librarian, I want a refused Manual charge to leave the Account exactly as it was, so that a mistake never half-applies.
+29. As a librarian, I want any reason text to be accepted, so that recording a charge is never held up by the reason field.
+
+### Payments
+
+30. As a Member, I want to pay some or all of what I owe, so that I can clear my Account.
+31. As a librarian, I want recording a Payment to return the Member's new Balance, so that I can tell them what's left.
+32. As a librarian, I want a Payment larger than the Balance to be refused as an invalid amount, so that the library never holds Member credit.
+33. As a librarian, I want a Payment from a Member who owes nothing to be refused as an invalid amount, so that money isn't taken by mistake.
+34. As a librarian, I want a Payment of zero, a negative amount, a fraction of a cent, a float, an integer or a non-finite number to be refused as an invalid amount, so that only real, exact payments are recorded.
+35. As a librarian, I want a Payment for an unknown Member to fail with "member not found" rather than "invalid amount", so that the more basic mistake is the one reported.
+36. As a librarian, I want a refused Payment to leave the Account exactly as it was, so that a mistake never half-applies.
+37. As a Member who is Blocked from borrowing, I want to still be able to pay, so that I can clear my Balance and borrow again.
+
+### Blocking checkout
+
+38. As a librarian, I want checkout refused with an "account blocked" error when the Member's Balance is at or above the Borrowing limit of 10.00, so that Members who owe too much can't borrow more.
+39. As a Member, I want a Balance of exactly 10.00 to block me and 9.99 not to, so that the limit is clear.
+40. As a Member, I want to be able to borrow again as soon as a Payment brings my Balance below the Borrowing limit, so that paying has an immediate effect.
+41. As a librarian, I want a refused checkout to change nothing (the copy stays on the shelf and no Loan is created), so that a refusal leaves no trace.
+42. As a Member who is Blocked from borrowing, I want to still be able to return copies, so that being blocked never stops copies coming back.
+43. As a librarian, I want checkout to report problems in a fixed order (unknown Member, unknown copy, Blocked from borrowing, copy not available, loan limit reached), so that the error shown is predictable.
+44. As a librarian, I want unknown Members to keep failing checkout with "member not found", so that existing behaviour doesn't change.
+45. As a librarian, I want a Fine charged by a return to count toward the Borrowing limit straight away, so that a Member who brings back a long-overdue DVD can be blocked at that same desk visit.
+46. As a librarian, I want Manual charges to count toward the Borrowing limit just like Fines, so that one Balance governs blocking.
+
+### Reports
+
+47. As a librarian, I want the overdue report to keep showing days overdue in calendar days as it does today, so that the printout I'm used to doesn't change.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this), all on `LendingService`:**
+  - `return_copy(copy_id)` keeps its signature and still returns the closed `Loan`. A late return also charges the Fine.
+  - `balance(member_id: str) -> Decimal`: the Member's current Balance.
+  - `add_charge(member_id: str, amount: Decimal, reason: str) -> Decimal`: adds a Manual charge and returns the new Balance.
+  - `pay(member_id: str, amount: Decimal) -> Decimal`: records a Payment and returns the new Balance.
+- **New errors** in the lending errors module, both subclasses of `LendingError`:
+  - `AccountBlocked`, raised by `checkout` when the Member is Blocked from borrowing.
+  - `InvalidAmount`, for amounts that `add_charge` or `pay` can't accept.
+  - Unknown Members keep raising `MemberNotFound`.
+- **Policy numbers live in the policy module**, as the README requires for circulation numbers: Fine rate per Material (book 0.25, DVD 1.00) and the Borrowing limit (10.00). Shape the limit so each action can have its own threshold (holds will later have a 5.00 Hold limit). Don't hard-code a single "blocked" flag.
+- **Late days**: count the days strictly after `due_on`, up to and including the return day, on which the library is open. Use the same open-day rule that due dates already use, so closed weekdays are defined in one place. Worked examples:
+  - Book due Mon 23 Mar 2026, returned Mon 30 Mar 2026: 24–30 Mar is 7 days, minus Sunday 29 Mar = 6 Late days = 1.50.
+  - DVD due Mon 9 Mar 2026, returned Mon 23 Mar 2026: 12 Late days = 12.00, which equals its replacement cost and so is also the cap.
+  - Returned on or before `due_on`: 0 Late days, no Fine.
+- **Fine amount** = Late days × rate for the Title's Material, capped so the **total fined for one Loan** never exceeds the Title's `replacement_cost`. The cap is per Loan, not per Fine. Today a Loan gets at most one Fine, but each Fine must be linked to its Loan so that renewals can enforce the per-Loan cap later.
+- **Fine calculation shape**: compute the Fine as "lateness of this Loan from its due date up to day X", rather than reading `returned_on` directly. Renewals will reuse it with X = the renewal day.
+- **Child exemption**: Members whose category is child are never charged automatic Charges (Fines now, No-show fees later). Write it as one rule about automatic Charges, not a check inside the return path. Manual charges apply to children like anyone else.
+- **No Account entry** for a zero Fine (on-time return) or for a child's late return.
+- **Account storage**: the Account holds Charges and Payments, and Balance is computed from them. Storage form is the implementer's choice. Recording entries (date, kind, amount, reason, and the Loan for Fines) is preferred over a running total, so that the per-Loan cap and future history are possible. The repository stays the only persistence. No public method beyond the four above.
+- **Balance semantics**: posted Charges minus Payments. Lateness on Loans still out does not count (ADR-0001). Balance is never negative.
+- **Amount validation** (both `add_charge` and `pay`). Reject with `InvalidAmount` when the amount:
+  - is not a `Decimal` (including `float` and `int`);
+  - is NaN or infinite;
+  - is zero or negative;
+  - has more than two decimal places.
+
+  `pay` also rejects any amount greater than the current Balance (including any amount when the Balance is zero).
+- **Error precedence**:
+  - `balance`, `add_charge` and `pay` check that the Member exists first (`MemberNotFound`), then validate the amount.
+  - `checkout` checks in this order: Member exists → copy exists → Blocked from borrowing → copy available → loan limit.
+- **Atomicity**: a refused `checkout`, `add_charge` or `pay` changes no state. Validate everything before mutating.
+- **Never blocked**: `return_copy` and `pay` work regardless of Balance.
+- **Reason text** on Manual charges is stored as given and not checked.
+- **Exports**: the package's existing public exports need no change beyond what the new errors require. The errors are imported from the errors module, as the existing ones are.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the `LendingService` public interface, driven by `FixedClock`.** Tests check out a copy, move the clock, return it, and assert on `balance`, return values and raised errors. Nothing tests the policy functions, the repository or Account storage directly. Those are internal details and must stay free to change.
+- A good test describes behaviour a librarian would recognise, e.g. "a book six Late days late costs 1.50" or "a Member owing exactly 10.00 can't borrow". Use concrete dates on the fixture's calendar (START is Monday 2 March 2026) so Sundays fall in known places.
+- **Prior art:** the existing loan tests use the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures (two Members, one adult and one child; a book Title with replacement cost 18.00 and two copies; a DVD Title with replacement cost 12.00 and one copy) and `pytest.raises` for domain errors. Follow the same style, and add a new test module for fines and Accounts.
+- **Behaviours to cover:**
+  - Fines: book and DVD rates; Sundays skipped (including a Loan spanning more than one Sunday); return on the due date and one day after; the per-Loan cap reached and exceeded; child late return charges nothing but still closes the Loan and shelves the copy; several late returns add up; `return_copy` still returns the Loan; an overdue Loan still out adds nothing to the Balance.
+  - Account: a new Member's Balance is zero; `add_charge` and `pay` return the new Balance; Manual charges on children; every `InvalidAmount` case for both operations; overpayment and paying with zero Balance; `MemberNotFound` for unknown Members on all three, and ahead of `InvalidAmount`; refused calls leave the Balance unchanged.
+  - Blocking: blocked at exactly 10.00 and not at 9.99; a Fine from a return can block the next checkout; paying below the limit unblocks; a refused checkout leaves the copy available and creates no Loan; a blocked Member can still return and pay; `AccountBlocked` takes precedence over `CopyNotAvailable` and `LoanLimitReached`; unknown Member and unknown copy still win over `AccountBlocked`.
+- The existing overdue report tests stay as they are, as a check that the report hasn't changed.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Holds, the Hold limit (5.00) and the No-show fee (1.00). The design leaves room for them; they are not built.
+- Renewals, including how the per-Loan cap and earlier lateness are handled across a renewal.
+- Waiving, reversing or refunding Charges; credit balances.
+- Blocking Members merely for having an overdue Loan still out.
+- Charges for lost or withdrawn copies.
+- A public Account history or statement method.
+- Validating or requiring the reason text on Manual charges.
+- Any change to the overdue report.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md` (Account, Charge, Fine, Manual charge, Payment, Balance, Borrowing limit, Blocked from borrowing, Late day). Say "Blocked from borrowing", never a bare "Blocked". Keep "Late days" (Fines) distinct from "days overdue" (the calendar-day count on the overdue report).
+- ADR-0001 (Fines are charged when the copy comes back, not accrued) governs Balance semantics. Renewals must later charge lateness up to the renewal day so that it never disappears.
+- Planned follow-ups that this work should not make harder: a Hold limit lower than the Borrowing limit, refused with the same `AccountBlocked`; a No-show fee on the same Balance that children never pay; a renewal Fine counted exactly as if the copy were returned on the renewal day, with lateness after that counted from the new due date.
