+# Renewals
+
+Status: ready-for-agent
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Members often ask to keep a book longer. There is no way to do that, so staff return the copy and check it straight back out to the same Member. That creates a second Loan with a new id and a new borrowed date, so the Member's loan history shows two Loans where there was really one. Any lateness on the first Loan is fined as a return that never physically happened. Nothing stops staff from doing this while other Members are waiting in the Hold queue for the Title, and nothing limits how often a book can be kept this way.
+
+## Solution
+
+Staff can renew an open Loan. The same Loan stays open with the same id and the same borrowed date, and gets a new due date: a full loan period counted from the day it is renewed. The Loan records how many times it has been renewed.
+
+Only book Loans can be renewed, at most twice each. DVDs can never be renewed. A Loan can be renewed only within its Renewal window, which opens five calendar days before its due date and stays open from then on, so an overdue Loan can still be renewed. A renewal is refused while anyone has a waiting Hold on the Title. It is also refused if the Member owes anything at all; they are Blocked from renewing until they pay.
+
+If the Loan is already late when it is renewed, the lateness so far is charged as a Fine on the renewal day, exactly as if the copy had come back that day (ADR-0001). Lateness after that is counted from the new due date. The per-Loan Fine cap covers every Fine on the Loan together. Child Members are never fined, as for any Fine. A refused renewal changes nothing.
+
+## User Stories
+
+### Renewing
+
+1. As a Member, I want to keep a book longer without handing it back, so that I can finish it.
+2. As a librarian, I want to renew a Loan by its id, so that I don't have to return and re-check out the copy.
+3. As a librarian, I want a renewal to keep the same Loan, so that the Member's loan history shows one Loan for one borrowing.
+4. As a librarian, I want renewing to return the renewed Loan, so that I can tell the Member the new due date.
+5. As a Member, I want the new due date to be a full loan period from the day I renew, so that I get a full period of extra time.
+6. As a librarian, I want the new due date to follow the same rule as at checkout, moving to Monday if it falls on a Sunday, so that a Loan is never due on a closed day.
+7. As a librarian, I want the Loan's borrowed date to stay the day the copy actually went out, so that the history shows when the Member really took it.
+8. As a librarian, I want each Loan to show how many times it has been renewed, so that I can tell the Member how many renewals they have left.
+9. As a librarian, I want a Loan that has never been renewed to show zero renewals, so that existing and new Loans read the same way.
+10. As a librarian, I want the copy to stay on loan to the same Member through a renewal, so that nobody else can borrow it or see it as available.
+11. As a Member, I want to renew a book a second time, so that I can keep a long book for a while longer.
+
+### What can be renewed
+
+12. As a librarian, I want a book Loan to be renewable at most twice, so that copies come back to the library eventually.
+13. As a librarian, I want a third renewal of a book Loan to be refused with a reason, so that I can explain the limit to the Member.
+14. As a librarian, I want DVD Loans never to be renewable, so that DVDs, with their short loan period, keep circulating.
+15. As a librarian, I want a DVD renewal to be refused with a reason, so that I can explain the rule to the Member.
+16. As a librarian, I want renewing a Loan that has already been returned to be refused with a reason, so that a closed Loan's due date can't change.
+17. As a librarian, I want renewing an unknown Loan id to fail as an unknown Loan, as it does elsewhere, so that typos are caught the same way everywhere.
+
+### The Renewal window
+
+18. As a librarian, I want a Loan to be renewable only from five calendar days before its due date, so that Members renew when they really need more time and not as soon as they borrow.
+19. As a librarian, I want a Loan due Monday 23 March to be renewable from Wednesday 18 March and refused on Tuesday 17 March, so that the window is easy to explain.
+20. As a librarian, I want a renewal before the window to be refused with a reason, so that I can tell the Member when to come back.
+21. As a Member, I want to be able to renew on the due date itself, so that I'm not penalised for coming in on the last day.
+22. As a Member, I want to be able to renew a Loan that is already overdue, so that I can still keep the book if nobody is waiting for it.
+23. As a librarian, I want a renewal on the checkout day to be refused, so that a Member can't use up a renewal without gaining any time.
+24. As a librarian, I want a second renewal on the same day as the first to be refused, so that a Member can't use up both renewals for one extension.
+25. As a Member, I want the window for a second renewal to count from the Loan's new due date, so that I can renew again near the end of the extended period.
+
+### Waiting Holds
+
+26. As a Member waiting in the Hold queue, I want a Loan on that Title not to be renewable while I'm waiting, so that I get a copy as soon as one comes back.
+27. As a librarian, I want a renewal refused because of a waiting Hold to say so, so that I can explain why to the Member.
+28. As a librarian, I want Ready Holds not to block renewals, so that a Member who already has a copy set aside for them doesn't stop someone else from renewing.
+29. As a librarian, I want renewals of a Title to become possible again once its Hold queue has no waiting Holds, so that the rule only applies while someone is actually waiting.
+30. As a librarian, I want a Hold on a different Title not to affect the renewal, so that only the Loan's own Title counts.
+
+### Owing nothing
+
+31. As a librarian, I want a Member who owes anything at all to be Blocked from renewing, so that renewals are a privilege for Members whose Account is clear.
+32. As a librarian, I want a Balance of even 0.01 to block renewing, so that the rule is simply "owes nothing".
+33. As a librarian, I want a renewal refused because of the Balance to be reported the same way as being Blocked from borrowing or holds, naming the action "renewing", so that the desk handles every Balance block the same way.
+34. As a Member, I want to be able to renew once I've paid my Balance off, so that paying lifts the block.
+35. As a librarian, I want a No-show fee or a Manual charge to block renewing until it's paid, so that every kind of Charge counts.
+36. As a librarian, I want being Blocked from renewing to leave returning, paying and borrowing below the Borrowing limit unaffected, so that the block only affects renewals.
+
+### Lateness at renewal
+
+37. As a librarian, I want renewing an overdue Loan to charge the Fine for the lateness so far, counted as if the copy came back that day, so that lateness before a renewal is never lost.
+38. As a librarian, I want the "owes nothing" check to look at the Balance before that renewal Fine, so that a Member who owes nothing can renew an overdue Loan and then owes its Fine.
+39. As a librarian, I want lateness after a renewal to count only from the new due date, so that no Late day is fined twice.
+40. As a librarian, I want Sundays not to count as Late days at renewal, as at return, so that the Fine is the same either way.
+41. As a librarian, I want the total fined for one Loan, across renewals and the final return, never to exceed its Title's replacement cost, so that the per-Loan cap still holds.
+42. As a librarian, I want each renewal Fine linked to its Loan and dated on the renewal day, so that the Account shows when and for what it was charged.
+43. As a librarian, I want child Members never to be fined at renewal, so that the child exemption applies to every Fine.
+44. As a librarian, I want renewing a Loan that isn't late to charge nothing, so that on-time renewals are free.
+45. As a librarian, I want a renewal Fine to count toward the Borrowing limit and the Hold limit straight away, so that it behaves like any other Fine.
+46. As a Member, I want to be told that I can't renew again until the renewal Fine is paid, so that I understand the second renewal is blocked.
+
+### Refusals
+
+47. As a librarian, I want a refused renewal to change nothing (no Fine, no new due date, no change to the renewal count), so that a failed attempt leaves the Loan and Account as they were.
+48. As a librarian, I want the reason that won't go away to be reported first when several apply, so that I don't tell a Member to pay when they would still be refused afterwards.
+
+### Unchanged behaviour
+
+49. As a librarian, I want a renewed Loan that is no longer past its due date to drop off the overdue list and the overdue report, so that the report shows what is really late.
+50. As a librarian, I want returning a renewed copy to behave exactly like any other return, passing the copy on to the Hold queue as usual, so that renewals don't change returns.
+51. As a librarian, I want checkouts, returns, Holds, Fines, Payments and the overdue report to behave exactly as before for Loans that are never renewed, so that renewals don't disturb anything else.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this).**
+  - `LendingService.renew(loan_id: str) -> Loan`: renews the open Loan in place (same `loan_id`) and returns it.
+  - `Loan` gets a field `renewals: int` with default `0`: how many times the Loan has been renewed. It is the last field, so existing construction is unaffected.
+  - New error `RenewalRefused(LendingError)` in the lending errors module, for renewals that can't be granted. Shaped like `HoldNotAllowed`: it carries the `loan_id` and a human-readable `reason`.
+  - Unknown loan ids keep raising `LoanNotFound`. Existing errors are reused where they fit: a Balance above zero raises `AccountBlocked`.
+- **`renew` checks in this order**, reports the first refusal, and a refusal changes nothing:
+  1. Loan exists (`LoanNotFound`).
+  2. Loan is open; an already-returned Loan is `RenewalRefused`.
+  3. Renewal allowance: a DVD Loan, or a book Loan already renewed twice, is `RenewalRefused`.
+  4. Renewal window: today earlier than the due date minus 5 calendar days is `RenewalRefused`.
+  5. Hold queue: any *waiting* Hold on the Loan's Title is `RenewalRefused`. Ready Holds don't count. The Member's own Hold can't be the cause, because a Member can't hold a Title they have on loan.
+  6. Not Blocked from renewing: a Balance at or above 0.01 is `AccountBlocked` with action "renewing" and limit 0.01.
+
+  The order puts reasons that won't go away first and the Balance last. Steps 3 to 5 each raise `RenewalRefused` with a different reason.
+- **Granting a renewal**, only after every check passes, in this order:
+  1. Charge any Fine for the lateness from the current due date up to today, using the same Fine calculation as a return with today as the end day. It goes through the same automatic-charge path, so the per-Loan cap (counting Fines already charged for this Loan), the child exemption and "no entry for a zero Fine" all apply unchanged. The Account entry is a Fine linked to the Loan and dated today.
+  2. Set the due date to the same due-date rule checkout uses, from today and the Title's Material.
+  3. Increment `renewals`.
+
+  `loan_id`, `copy_id`, `member_id`, `borrowed_on` and `returned_on` (still empty) don't change. The copy stays on loan. Nothing else is recorded about the renewal: the count, plus the dated Fine entry if there was one, is the whole record.
+- **Policy numbers** live in the policy module with the other circulation numbers:
+  - The renewal allowance per Material: books 2, DVDs 0. One table covers both "DVDs can't be renewed" and the cap. It is the same for adults and children.
+  - The Renewal window: 5 calendar days before the due date. Sundays count; this is not an open-day count.
+  - The Balance limit for the new "renewing" action is 0.01, added next to the Borrowing limit and Hold limit and checked through the same "blocked from" check checkout and `place_hold` use.
+- **No separate "the due date wouldn't move" rule.** The window makes it unnecessary. A book's due date is at least 21 days after checkout or a renewal, so the window opens no earlier than 16 days later. That rules out renewing on the checkout day or twice in one day.
+- **Worked examples** on the test fixture's calendar (START is Monday 2 March 2026; Sundays are 8, 15, 22, 29 March and 5, 12, 19 April):
+  - A book borrowed Monday 2 March is due Monday 23 March. Renewing on Tuesday 17 March is refused (before the window). Renewing on Wednesday 18 March is granted: the new due date is Wednesday 8 April, renewals 1, no Fine. The window for a second renewal opens Friday 3 April.
+  - The same Loan renewed overdue on Saturday 28 March: Late days 24 to 28 March = 5, so the Fine is 1.25, posted 28 March. The new due date is Saturday 18 April. If it is then returned on Tuesday 21 April, the Late days are 20 and 21 April (Sunday 19 April doesn't count), so a further 0.50, 1.75 in total for the Loan.
+  - Renewed overdue on Monday 30 March: Late days 24 to 28 and 30 March = 6 (Sunday 29 March doesn't count), so the Fine is 1.50. The new due date is Monday 20 April.
+  - The child Member renewing the same overdue Loan on 28 March is charged nothing and the renewal is granted.
+  - Ann (owing nothing) renews her overdue Loan on 28 March and now owes 1.25. A second renewal is refused with `AccountBlocked` until she pays it, even once the window for the new due date opens.
+- **Overdue listings.** `overdue_loans` and the overdue report read the Loan's current due date, so a renewed Loan is overdue only after its new due date, and its days overdue count from that date. No code change.
+- **Persistence.** Renewal mutates the stored Loan in place; there is no new record and no new repository operation. Only the service changes Loans.
+- **Atomicity.** Validate everything before mutating; a refused renewal posts no Fine and leaves `due_on` and `renewals` as they were.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the `LendingService` public interface, driven by `FixedClock`.** Tests check out and renew Loans, move the clock, place Holds, add charges and payments, return copies, and assert on the `Loan` returned by `renew` and by `get_loan`, `loan_history`, `open_loans`, `overdue_loans`, `balance`, `get_copy`, `holds_for`, and on raised errors. Nothing tests the policy functions, the models or the repository directly; those are internal details and must stay free to change. Fine amounts and the cap are tested only through the Balance.
+- A good test describes behaviour a librarian would recognise, e.g. "a book due Monday 23 March can be renewed from Wednesday 18 March" or "renewing on Saturday 28 March charges 1.25 for five Late days". Use concrete dates on the fixture's calendar.
+- **Prior art:** the existing holds and fines tests use the shared `service` and `clock` fixtures (an adult `ann` and a child `kid`; book Title `978-1` with copies B1 and B2 at replacement cost 18.00; DVD Title `dvd-1` with copy D1) and `pytest.raises` for domain errors. Follow the same style in a new test module for renewals. Register extra Members inside tests where a Hold queue needs more people. Add a cheap Title inside a test where the per-Loan cap has to be reached in a reasonable number of days.
+- **Behaviours to cover:**
+  - Granting: same `loan_id`; returned Loan is the stored one; new due date from the renewal day; `borrowed_on` unchanged; `renewals` goes 0 → 1 → 2; copy still on loan; `loan_history` still shows one Loan; a new Loan starts at 0 renewals.
+  - Allowance: a third renewal refused; a DVD refused even inside its window and with nothing else in the way, with `renewals` still 0.
+  - Window: refused 6 days before the due date, granted 5 days before, on the due date and when overdue; refused on the checkout day; a second renewal's window counts from the new due date.
+  - Holds: refused while a waiting Hold is on the Title; granted when the only Hold on the Title is Ready; granted again after the waiting Hold is cancelled or becomes Ready; a Hold on another Title doesn't block it.
+  - Balance: refused with `AccountBlocked` (action "renewing") at a Balance of 0.01; granted at 0.00 after paying; a No-show fee blocks it.
+  - Lateness: overdue renewal charges the Fine as if returned that day, with Sundays not counted; no Fine for an on-time renewal; no Fine for a child; a later return fines only the days after the new due date; the per-Loan cap holds across a renewal Fine and the return Fine; a renewal Fine can block borrowing or holds.
+  - Order and atomicity: `LoanNotFound` for an unknown id; a returned Loan refused; a reason that won't go away beats the Balance (e.g. a DVD renewal by a Member who owes money raises `RenewalRefused`, not `AccountBlocked`); a waiting Hold beats the Balance; every refusal leaves the due date, the renewal count and the Balance unchanged.
+  - Overdue: a renewed Loan no longer overdue drops off `overdue_loans`.
+- The existing loan, catalogue, fines and holds tests stay as they are, as a check that nothing else changed.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Renewing DVDs, or any per-Member-category difference in the renewal allowance.
+- Members renewing for themselves (online, by phone); this is the desk operation the other channels would call.
+- Automatic renewals, renewing all of a Member's Loans at once, and renewal reminders or notices.
+- A renewal fee.
+- A dated list of each renewal or of earlier due dates on the Loan.
+- Staff overriding a refusal, undoing a renewal, or setting a due date by hand.
+- Refusing renewals on days the library is closed (checkout doesn't check this either).
+- Any change to the overdue report's format or to how Fines are calculated beyond adding the renewal day as an end day.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md`: Renewal, Renewal window, Blocked from renewing, Fine, Late day, Balance, Hold queue, waiting, Ready. Avoid "extension", "re-checkout" and "renewal period". Never say a bare "Blocked".
+- **ADR-0001 (Fines charged on return)** applies as its Consequences planned. A renewal charges the lateness so far, counted as if the copy came back that day, so lateness before a renewal never disappears. Between events, a Loan still out adds nothing to the Balance.
+- This resolves the question the holds spec left open: the "owes nothing" check comes **before** the renewal Fine. An overdue Loan can be renewed by a Member who owes nothing, who then owes the Fine.
+- The fines work already prepared for this: the Fine calculation takes an end day, and each Fine is linked to its Loan so the cap is enforced per Loan across several Fines.
